# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT BUILT OR PUBLISHED BY ANY PIPELINE TODAY. Read this before changing that.
#
# docker-compose.yml pulls the redis image directly, so the CUSTOMER obtains it
# from its upstream registry and Firekeep never redistributes it. Building this
# Dockerfile and publishing the result to any registry reverses that: Firekeep
# becomes a redistributor of the bundled software, and its licence obligations
# attach to us rather than passing through.
#
# This file used to say `redis:7-alpine`, and that floating tag is how the
# LICENCE of what we reference moved without a commit: it now resolves to
# **7.4.10**, PAST Redis's 7.4 relicensing. 7.4.10 is RSALv2 or SSPLv1 (dual,
# source-available, NOT OSI open source), not the old BSD terms. RSALv2 forbids
# offering the software to third parties as a managed service. Self-hosted-by-
# the-customer is fine; a hosted Firekeep offering would not be.
#
# Source for that: redis/redis LICENSE.txt at tag 7.4.10 (upstream, the exact
# version pinned below). NOT the image — this image bundles no licence file at
# all, which is itself worth knowing before redistributing it.
#
# Do not wire this into a release pipeline without reading
# docs/THIRD-PARTY-DATASTORES.md and getting a legal read. Shipping an
# appliance, OVA or all-in-one installer has the same effect.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned by tag AND digest (multi-arch manifest list) so the terms above stay
# true of what this file actually references. A floating tag cannot express
# "we referenced BSD Redis" — the statement has no fixed subject. A digest can.
FROM redis:7.4.10-alpine@sha256:e7723ff73d963f5cc6d9c4643ea3d989527a402a319239054e9472a7fb9219a2
